So if there is an edge $i \to A_i$, then $x_i \le x_{A_i}$.
If there is a cycle $i_1 \to i_2 \to \dots \to i_k \to i_1$, then $x_{i_1} \le x_{i_2} \le \dots \le x_{i_k} \le x_{i_1}$, so all $x$ values in the cycle must be equal.
Also, if there is a path from $u$ to $v$, then $x_u \le x_v$.
If there is a path from $v$ to $u$, then $x_v \le x_u$, so $x_u = x_v$.
So, if $u$ and $v$ are in the same Strongly Connected Component (SCC), then $x_u = x_v$.
Let's condense the graph into its SCCs. Let the SCCs be $C_1, C_2, \dots, C_k$.
If there is an edge from a node in $C_a$ to a node in $C_b$, then for any $u \in C_a, v \in C_b$, we have $x_u \le x_v$. Since all nodes in $C_a$ have the same value and all nodes in $C_b$ have the same value, this implies $val(C_a) \le val(C_b)$.
The condensation graph is a Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG).
Let $y_j$ be the value assigned to all nodes in SCC $C_j$. The condition becomes $y_j \le y_l$ if there is an edge from $C_j$ to $C_l$ in the condensation graph.
Also, for each node $i$, $1 \le x_i \le M$. This means $1 \le y_j \le M$ for all $j$.
So the problem reduces to counting the number of assignments of values $y_1, \dots, y_k$ to the SCCs such that $1 \le y_j \le M$ and $y_j \le y_l$ for every edge $C_j \to C_l$ in the DAG.

