The problem asks us to count the number of "Snake numbers" in a given range $[L, R]$.
A Snake number is defined as a positive integer $\ge 10$ such that its most significant digit (MSD) is strictly greater than every other digit in the number.
The constraints are $10 \le L \le R \le 10^{18}$.

Let $N$ be a number. Let $d_k d_{k-1} \dots d_1 d_0$ be its decimal representation, where $d_k$ is the MSD.
The condition is $d_k > d_i$ for all $0 \le i < k$.
This implies that all digits $d_{k-1}, \dots, d_0$ must be strictly less than $d_k$.
