The problem asks us to find the number of distinct pairs of mochi $(A, B)$ such that we can stack $A$ on top of $B$. The condition for stacking $A$ on top of $B$ is that the size of $A$ must be at most half the size of $B$. That is, $A \le B/2$, or equivalently $2A \le B$.
We are given $N$ mochi with sizes $A_1, A_2, \dots, A_N$. The sizes are sorted in ascending order: $A_1 \le A_2 \le \dots \le A_N$.
We need to count the number of pairs of indices $(i, j)$ with $1 \le i, j \le N$ such that $A_i$ can be placed on top of $A_j$. Note that the problem statement says "You choose two mochi out of the N mochi". Usually, this implies distinct physical mochi. However, the problem also says "Two kagamimochi are distinguished if at least one of the mochi is different, even if the sizes of the mochi are the same." This phrasing is slightly ambiguous. Does it mean we are choosing two distinct indices $i$ and $j$? Or does it mean we are choosing two mochi from the available set?
